+### Iterating with `foreach`
+
+A `foreach` statement runs its block once for each item in an iteration source.
+
+To iterate over the matches of a pattern on the current line:
+
+```
+counter words
+foreach /\w+/ {
+  words++
+}
+```
+
+The block runs once per non-overlapping match, left to right, so `words` is
+increased by the number of words on each line. Inside the block the capture
+groups of the pattern are bound to the current match. Add `in expr` to match
+within the string value of an expression instead of the whole line:
+
+```
+foreach /(?P<pair>\w+)/ in $csv {
+  ACTION
+}
+```
+
+An optional trailing `else` block runs when the loop performed no iterations; a
+foreach that performs no iterations counts as unmatched, so a following
+`otherwise` in the same scope still runs.
+
+To iterate over the stored entries of a single-dimension metric, in ascending
+key order:
+
+```
+counter total
+/DONE/ {
+  foreach k in bytes_by_operation {
+    total += bytes_by_operation[k]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The `foreach key, value in metric` form also binds the entry value, and adding
+`limit n` visits only the `n` entries with the largest values. The keys are
+snapshotted when the loop begins. To iterate over the fields of a string split
+on a separator pattern, use `foreach field in fields(expr, /sep/)`.
+
+Two builtin functions support iteration: `matchindex()` returns the zero-based
+index of the current iteration of the innermost enclosing `foreach` (valid only
+inside a foreach body), and `matchcount(/pattern/)` returns the number of
+matches of `pattern` on the current line.
+
